{%- comment -%}
  payment-icons.liquid — the gateways the shop has actually enabled.

  §9.6: never a hardcoded image strip. `shop.enabled_payment_types` is the
  only source, so the icons are always true for the store rendering them.
  A shop with nothing enabled renders nothing rather than an empty list.

  The list is decorative reassurance next to a purchase action, so it is
  labelled for assistive tech and the individual marks are not announced.

  `payment_type_svg_tag` emits fixed ids — `id="pi-visa"`, and gradient defs
  like `id="pi-paint0_linear"` referenced by `url(#pi-paint0_linear)`. Render
  the row twice on one page (the product page trust row and the footer both
  want it) and every one of those ids is duplicated. That is invalid HTML, it
  can make the second SVG's gradient resolve against the first, and Lighthouse
  flags it under "[id] attributes are not unique".

  Shopify's filter offers no way to namespace them, so `id_prefix` rewrites
  the ids and their references. Two substring replacements cover every case:
  `"pi-` catches both `id="pi-…"` and `aria-labelledby="pi-…"`, and `(#pi-`
  catches the `url(#pi-…)` fill references.

  Params:
    class      (string, optional)
    id_prefix  (string, optional) — required on any second row in one document
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if shop.enabled_payment_types.size > 0 -%}
  <ul class="payment-icons {{ class }}" aria-label="{{ 'footer.payment_methods' | t }}">
    {%- for type in shop.enabled_payment_types -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign mark = type | payment_type_svg_tag: class: 'payment-icons__icon'
        if id_prefix != blank
          assign id_to = '"' | append: id_prefix | append: 'pi-'
          assign ref_to = '(#' | append: id_prefix | append: 'pi-'
          assign mark = mark | replace: '"pi-', id_to | replace: '(#pi-', ref_to
        endif
      -%}
      <li>{{ mark }}</li>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </ul>
{%- endif -%}
